WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.100
- A common complaint
that math teachers hear

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:06.180
is when am I gonna need
this in the real world?

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.013
I get it.

00:00:07.013 --> 00:00:08.070
When I was in high school,

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.650
I felt like algebra class was pointless.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.720
A bunch of imaginary numbers and rules

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.450
dreamed up by philosophers
trying to prove to each other

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:16.920
how smart they were.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.680
- But the origins of math
are deeply practical.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.020
Many mathematical innovations
were invented to solve

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:25.890
very real world problems--problems
of resource distribution,

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.830
of labor compensation, trade, property,

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:31.530
of determining the very value of things.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:33.300
In other words, finance.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.390
- And even though we, modern
humans, have calculators

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:39.180
and accountants to crunch
the big numbers for us,

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.060
it still takes an understanding
of math to really know

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:46.404
what your money is doing and
what risks are worth taking.

00:00:46.404 --> 00:00:48.987
(bright music)

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.210
To help explain how money influenced

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:01.140
the development of mathematics,
we invited Naiomi Israel,

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:04.830
a program director with the
nonprofit organization FiCycle,

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:08.670
which teaches high school
kids math through finance.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:11.280
- Since the beginning of the species,

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.070
Homo sapiens have had to
learn how to work together

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:16.080
and share the results of that work.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.330
If two families of early people managed

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.250
to bring down a wooly mammoth,

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.610
how should they split the meat
so that no one gets angry?

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:28.560
The idea of fairness was essential
to a cooperative society,

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.160
which required methods to
measure and count things.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.680
One of the earliest numbering systems

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.990
was known as a tally stick,

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:40.710
a piece of wood or bone
with notches carved into it.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.830
Some date back over 10,000 years!

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.650
The people who used them
probably didn't have words

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:49.050
or even concepts for numbers.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.690
Each notch likely corresponded to an item,

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:54.120
so they could keep track
of their possessions.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.070
- Once humans developed agriculture,

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:59.580
this concept was refined with
the invention of the bullae.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.110
Instead of notches in wood,

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.840
Sumerian farmers used small clay tokens

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.190
to represent their goods.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.860
For instance, if several
farmers were going to store

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.530
their grain together, they'd
use little sack tokens

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.440
with markings to identify
who's were whose.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.270
These tokens might be said to represent

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:18.240
the first form of math and money.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.510
- To prevent any funny
business, the tokens were sealed

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:25.230
in a hollow clay ball
called a bulla and baked,

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.830
so it would have to be broken
open to validate the contents.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:32.370
Eventually, they started
pressing impressions of the token

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:33.990
on the outside of the ball,

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:37.500
so it would only have to be
cracked in case of a dispute.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.440
It wasn't a big leap from here to realize

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.030
that they could press the tokens

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.250
into a separate clay tablet,

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.650
and then save room with new symbols

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:50.310
that represented numbers
known as cuneiform script,

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:53.790
and thus the accounting ledger was born.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.040
- Over the next few thousand years,

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.980
Babylonian mathematics,
as it's known today,

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:01.170
made many important discoveries,

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:04.350
most of which were inspired
by financial needs.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.960
If a toolmaker and a
shepherd wanted to trade,

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.120
they could use conversion
ratios to find out

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.420
how much each one's goods were
worth in a standardized unit,

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:14.160
like of sacks of grain.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.680
For tax purposes, the
government had to know

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.570
how much land someone owned,

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:21.030
so they developed sophisticated ways

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:22.770
of measuring irregular areas.

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:24.090
The resulting discipline,

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:27.750
Geometry, literally
means earth measurement.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:30.330
And if you needed to
split 20 loaves of bread

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:33.000
amongst nine workers,
you can't do it evenly

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.700
without inventing fractions.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:38.820
- By about 2000 BC, the
people of Mesopotamia

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:41.160
had started using precious
metals as currency

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:44.220
instead of cumbersome
cows and sacks of grain.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:47.940
But metals can't be counted
like cows or measured by area

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:50.280
like land or volume like grain.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.500
Their value is determined by weight,

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.420
which means they had to devise methods

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:58.560
to accurately weigh things
and create standardized units.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:01.950
- As trade exploded across
Europe, Asia, and Africa,

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:05.220
many different civilizations
began striking their own coins

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.740
of different metals and levels of purity.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:11.130
This may have simplified the
transportation of currency,

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:13.260
but it was a mathematical headache.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:16.320
For instance, if you have
a four gram silver coin

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:20.580
at 70% purity, a 2 gram
coin at 90% purity,

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:23.640
and a 5 gram coin at 60% purity,

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.890
and you melt them all
down and combine them,

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:28.740
how pure is the resulting lump of silver?

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:31.290
- Problems like these
spurred the development

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:33.090
of Hindu-Arabic mathematics

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:35.100
around the 9th or 10th century.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.860
The scholar al-Khwarizmi
provided equations

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:40.740
or sets of step-by-step instructions

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.890
to discover unknown
values from known values.

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:47.670
Algebra is derived from
the Arabic term al-jabr.

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:49.140
And the name al-Khwarizmi

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:51.990
eventually gave us the term algorithm

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:53.790
to describe a set of instructions

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:56.490
that provide an answer from an input.

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:59.400
Hindu scholars may also
have invented the concept

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.910
of negative numbers to represent debt.

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:04.290
- A couple hundred years later,

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:07.577
the son of an Italian merchant
named Leonardo Pisano,

00:05:07.577 --> 00:05:09.990
advocated Hindu-Arabic mathematics

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:12.090
for many financial purposes.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:14.220
Thanks to his book, "Liber Abaci",

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:17.490
Algebra spread throughout
the mercantile class in Italy

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:20.310
used for things like establishing
currency exchange rates

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:23.310
and calculating compound
interest on loans.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:25.470
Fibonacci, as he is more widely known,

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:28.560
is also credited with
developing present value:

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:30.330
the notion that theoretical money

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.190
in the future is worth less

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.350
than the same amount
of money in your hand.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:36.420
- Some fields of applied mathematics

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:38.550
also derive from financial needs.

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:41.850
Mathematicians like Girolamo
Cardano, Blaise Pascal,

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:45.090
and Pierre De Fermat
developed gambling strategies

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:48.390
that became the foundation
of probability theory,

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:51.240
and the science of statistics
was greatly advanced

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.490
by the needs of the insurance industry

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:55.110
looking to minimize risk.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:58.050
- Even calculus owes a
bit of debt to finance.

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:00.510
To save money on wine for his wedding,

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:03.000
Johannes Kepler came up with new equations

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:05.880
to find the exact
volumes of barrel shapes,

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.090
which contributed to the
development of integral calculus.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:12.720
- Today, virtually, everyone
has access to the innovations

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:15.120
of these mathematical pioneers,

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:17.640
but it wasn't always this way.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.260
Through much of ancient history,

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:21.990
the secrets of math were closely guarded,

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:23.250
and the elite who knew them

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:26.040
enjoyed special power and privilege.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:29.040
Math was the magic that moved money,

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:32.100
and money made the world go round.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:34.290
- I suppose that's kind of true today.

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:36.780
The "Wizards of Wall
Street" use advanced math

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:39.060
to influence the flow
of trillions of dollars

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.790
that affect people on
every corner of the globe.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:45.180
But you don't have to understand
derivative pricing formulas

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:47.580
to benefit financially from math.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:49.560
- The math you learn
in high school algebra

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:52.530
is all you need to make many
of the financial decisions

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:54.630
you'll face in life like investing,

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:56.940
budgeting, and managing risk.

00:06:56.940 --> 00:06:59.190
- Sure, you can have a financial advisor

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:02.370
do a lot of that work for you,
but at the end of the day,

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:04.890
if you really wanna
translate your life goals

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:06.690
into financial results,

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:09.926
it helps to speak the language of math.

00:07:09.926 --> 00:07:11.820
- [Julia and Philip] And
that's our Two Cents.

